# Adapters

An adapter is what delivers a message. `howdy_mail` comes with four:

- `howdy/mail/smtp` delivers over SMTP, to your provider's relay or a
  local Mailpit. Configure it with `smtp.from_env()`, which reads
  `SMTP_URL`, or `smtp.from_url(url)`.
- `howdy/mail/resend` delivers with [Resend](https://resend.com)'s HTTP
  API. Configure it with `resend.from_env()`, which reads
  `RESEND_API_KEY`, or `resend.new(key)`.
- `howdy/mail/sendgrid` delivers with [SendGrid](https://sendgrid.com)'s
  v3 Mail Send API. Configure it with `sendgrid.from_env()`, which reads
  `SENDGRID_API_KEY`, or `sendgrid.new(key)`.
- `howdy/mail/outbox` delivers nowhere: it keeps the message. See
  [Outbox and previews](/docs/mail/development).

Each one gives you an adapter to put in a mailer:

```gleam
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/resend

pub fn mailer() -> mail.Mailer {
  let assert Ok(config) = resend.from_env()
  mail.mailer(resend.adapter(config))
  |> mail.default_from(mail.named("Acme", "hello@acme.test"))
}
```

The rest of your app only sees the `Mailer`, so changing provider changes
one function.

## SMTP

```gleam
import howdy/mail/smtp

let assert Ok(config) = smtp.from_env()
let adapter = smtp.adapter(config)
```

`SMTP_URL` looks like `smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587`.
Percent-encode an `@` in the user name as `%40`. `from_env` returns
`Error(smtp.MissingUrl(variable: "SMTP_URL"))` when it is unset, and
`Error(smtp.InvalidUrl)` when it cannot be read. The URL is not repeated in
the error, because it may hold a password.

The scheme chooses the encryption:

- `smtps://` is TLS from the first byte, on port 465 unless the URL gives
  another.
- `smtp://` connects in plain text and requires an upgrade with
  `STARTTLS`, on port 587 unless the URL gives another. A server that does
  not offer the upgrade is refused rather than used unencrypted.
- `smtp://` to a loopback address, such as `localhost`, or to a name
  without a dot, such as a Compose service called `mailpit`, has TLS off.

Certificates are checked against the system's CAs and the host name. Add
`?tls=none`, `?tls=starttls` or `?tls=implicit` to the URL to choose
yourself.

The configuration can also be built in code:

```gleam
smtp.new("smtp.example.com")
|> smtp.port(2525)
|> smtp.credentials(username: "apikey", password: password)
|> smtp.tls(smtp.StartTls(verify: True))
|> smtp.timeout(5000)
```

`smtp.tls` takes `smtp.NoTls`, `smtp.StartTls(verify:)` or
`smtp.ImplicitTls(verify:)`. Only turn `verify` off for a server you
control. Without `credentials`, no authentication is tried. `timeout` is
how long to wait for the connection to open, 10 seconds by default (once
connected, each reply is waited for up to gen_smtp's own limit of twenty
minutes), and `helo` sets the name this host gives in `EHLO`.

Each send opens a connection, delivers one message and closes it, in the
process that called `mail.send`. An SMTP `4xx` reply is `Unavailable`, and
a `5xx` is `Refused`.

A temporary failure, which is an `Unavailable` one, can be tried again
before it is returned:

```gleam
smtp.from_env()
|> result.map(smtp.retries(_, 2))
|> result.map(smtp.backoff(_, 1000))
```

`retries` is how many more attempts to make, 0 by default, and `backoff`
is how long to wait before the first retry in milliseconds, 500 by default;
each retry after it waits twice as long. A `Refused` message is never tried
again. Every attempt happens in the calling process, so a send can take up
to `retries + 1` times `timeout`, plus the waits in between: see
"Sending is synchronous" in the [overview](/docs/mail/overview).

## Resend

```gleam
import howdy/mail/resend

let assert Ok(config) = resend.from_env()
let adapter = resend.adapter(config)
```

The sender's domain must be verified with Resend. Each message's id is sent
as the `Idempotency-Key`, so retrying the same message after a timeout does
not send it twice.

Resend only allows letters, digits, `_` and `-` in tags, so other
characters in a `mail.tag` become `_`: `auth.sign_in` arrives as
`auth_sign_in`.

## SendGrid

```gleam
import howdy/mail/sendgrid

let assert Ok(config) = sendgrid.from_env()
let adapter = sendgrid.adapter(config)
```

SendGrid needs at least one `To` recipient, and refuses an address that
appears twice, so an address already in `To` is dropped from `Cc` and
`Bcc`. Tags are sent as categories, of which SendGrid keeps the first ten.
The message's id is sent as the custom argument `howdy_id`.

For the EU region, change the base URL:

```gleam
sendgrid.new(api_key)
|> sendgrid.base_url("https://api.eu.sendgrid.com")
```

Both HTTP adapters wait 15 seconds by default; change it with
`resend.timeout` or `sendgrid.timeout`, in milliseconds. A `408`, `429` or `5xx`
response is `Unavailable`, and any other failure is `Refused`.

## Staging and fallbacks

Adapters wrap other adapters.

`mail.redirect_all` sends everything to one inbox, for a staging server
that must not mail real people:

```gleam
smtp.adapter(config)
|> mail.redirect_all(to: mail.address("staging@acme.test"))
```

`Cc` and `Bcc` are dropped, and the intended recipients are kept in an
`X-Original-To` header.

`mail.fallback` tries a second adapter when the first is `Unavailable`:

```gleam
mail.fallback(resend.adapter(resend_config), smtp.adapter(smtp_config))
```

A message the first adapter `Refused` is not tried again, since the second
would most likely refuse it too.

## Writing your own

An adapter is a name and a function from a checked `mail.Outgoing` to a
`mail.Receipt`:

```gleam
import gleam/option.{Some}
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/mime

pub fn adapter(config: Config) -> mail.Adapter {
  mail.adapter(named: "Postmark", send: fn(outgoing: mail.Outgoing) {
    // outgoing.from, .to, .cc, .bcc, .reply_to, .subject, .html, .text,
    // .headers, .attachments, .tags, .id and .date are all there.
    // mime.encode(outgoing) is the raw message, for APIs that take one.
    case post(config, outgoing) {
      Ok(provider_id) -> Ok(mail.Receipt(outgoing.id, Some(provider_id)))
      Error(Timeout) -> Error(mail.Unavailable("Postmark timed out"))
      Error(Rejected(reason)) -> Error(mail.Refused(reason))
    }
  })
}
```

The `Outgoing` has already passed every check, and has the mailer's
default sender. Return `Unavailable` for anything worth retrying, and
`Refused` for the rest, so `mail.retryable` and `mail.fallback` work.

`howdy/mail/mime` writes the message as the text an SMTP server receives:
`mime.encode(outgoing)` gives the whole of it, headers and body, which is
also what providers such as Amazon SES take as a raw message. `Bcc`
recipients are left out of it, since they belong only in the envelope;
`mail.recipients(outgoing)` lists every recipient for that.

`mail.deliver(adapter, outgoing)` hands an `Outgoing` to another adapter,
for adapters that wrap one.

## Testing a provider

`resend.request` and `sendgrid.request` build the HTTP request the adapter
would send, and `resend.receipt` and `sendgrid.receipt` read a response, so
you can test what your messages look like to a provider without the
network:

```gleam
import gleam/http/request
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/resend

pub fn welcome_request_test() {
  let assert Ok(outgoing) = mail.prepare(mailer(), welcome(user()))
  let assert Ok(req) = resend.request(resend.new("re_test"), outgoing)
  assert request.get_header(req, "idempotency-key") == Ok(outgoing.id)
}
```

To test the flows that send mail, use the
[outbox](/docs/mail/development) instead.
